

{"id":"0eeaa1cb-80e8-4011-9ff5-fec5c7278c44","object":"chat.completion","created":1786439777,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_024\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 6,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_07\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 11,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_05\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 9,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 6,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_09\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 17,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce one complete repair. The baseline decisions are given, but they are for the baseline scenario. The intervention includes overrides: initial_soc_overrides for UAV_01 (1.656781 kWh instead of 2.731279), closed_corridor_slots for COR_07 (slots 9-32) and COR_06 (slots 6-29). Also corridor_capacity_overrides: COR_01 slot 6 capacity 1.\n\nThe task says: \"Respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order\". So each mission is pre-assigned to a specific UAV: MISSION_01 and MISSION_02 to UAV_01, MISSION_03, MISSION_04, MISSION_05 to UAV_02. And sequence_index: For UAV_01: MISSION_01 index 0, MISSION_02 index 1. For UAV_02: MISSION_04 index 0, MISSION_03 index 1, MISSION_05 index 2. The order must be respected.\n\nWe need to choose a path for each mission, departure slot, charge blocks (0-3). Held may be true if we cannot serve? But we can try to schedule all without holds, as the baseline had no holds. The baseline departure slots: UAV_01 M01 slot 6 PATH_06, M02 slot 9 PATH_07. UAV_02 M04 slot 6 PATH_04, M03 slot 9 PATH_03, M05 slot 17 PATH_09.\n\nNow with interventions, some paths are blocked. For MISSION_01, origin VP_04, destination VP_05. Path options: PATH_02 (travel slots 3, corridor COR_05, wind 1), PATH_06 (travel slots 2, corridor COR_06, wind 1), PATH_08 (travel slots 2, corridor COR_01, wind 1). COR_06 is closed slots 6-29, so PATH_06 cannot be used because its travel occupies slots? The path travel slots = 2. If departure slot is 6, then slot 6 and 7 would be used. Check closed slots: COR_06 closed 6-29, so dep slot 6 path 06 would use slots 6-7, both closed, so not allowed. Could we depart later? But release slot is 6, due slot 8. So we need to depart at 6 or later? Release slot means earliest departure slot. Due slot 8 means mission must arrive by slot 8? Arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. For PATH_06 (2 slots), arriving at slot 8 means depart 6; depart 7 would arrive slot 9 > due 8. So cannot depart later due to due slot. So PATH_06 is infeasible. PATH_08 uses COR_01, travel 2 slots. COR_01 has capacity override: capacity 1 at slot 6. So only one mission can use COR_01 at slot 6. Also no closure. PATH_02 uses COR_05, travel 3 slots. Departing slot 6 would use slots 6,7,8? Wait travel_slots=3, so slots 6,7,8, arriving slot 8. That meets due slot 8. COR_05 is not closed, capacity 2 by default. So both PATH_08 and PATH_02 are possible. But we need to check energy and other constraints.\n\nUAV_01 initial SOC: from override: 1.656781 kWh. Battery capacity 16.0 kWh, reserve 1.5 kWh. Incoming deadhead: null. So no deadhead energy. Service flight energy: depends on path. For PATH_02: energy_kwh = 0.118406. For PATH_08: 0.108372. So after service, SOC = 1.656781 - 0.118406 = 1.538375 (if PATH_02). Then reserve check: SOC >= 1.5? Yes, 1.538375 > 1.5, ok. For PATH_08: 1.656781 - 0.108372 = 1.548409, also > 1.5. So both okay. Then turnaround: 1 slot. Then post-service charge: charge_blocks can be chosen, but at VP_05, charger capacity 2 slots default, no overrides. So we can charge. But note: MISSION_02 is next for UAV_01 from VP_05 to VP_02, release slot 9, due slot 24. We need to ensure enough energy for MISSION_02 after charging. So we'll likely charge to full or enough. So PATH_02 or PATH_08? Let's see corridor capacity constraints. At slot 6, PATH_08 uses COR_01, capacity 1 overridden. So only one mission can use COR_01 at slot 6. UAV_02 MISSION_04 also departs slot 6 from VP_04 to VP_05, path options: only PATH_04, which uses COR_01 and travel slots 1. That mission also uses COR_01, departure slot 6, so would use slot 6 only. If both M01 and M04 use COR_01 at slot 6, capacity 1 would be exceeded. But COR_01's capacity override is 1 at slot 6. So only one can use it. So we must avoid conflict. M04 only has PATH_04, which uses COR_01. So M04 must use COR_01 at slot 6. Therefore MISSION_01 cannot use PATH_08 (COR_01) at slot 6 because capacity 1. Could M01 depart at slot 7? But due slot 8, and travel 2 slots, depart 7 arrive 9 > due, so not allowed. So M01 must use PATH_02 (COR_05) or PATH_06, but PATH_06 closed. So M01 must use PATH_02, departing slot 6. That uses COR_05, capacity 2, no conflicts with M04. So MISSION_01: uav_id UAV_01, held false, path_id PATH_02, depart_slot 6, charge_blocks ? We'll decide later.\n\nNow MISSION_04: UAV_02, sequence 0, origin VP_04 to VP_05, path only PATH_04 (COR_01, travel 1 slot). Departure slot 6 (release 6, due 29). Travel 1 slot, so arrival slot 6. That uses COR_01 slot 6, capacity 1 allowed. Check energy: UAV_02 initial SOC 10.65855 kWh, capacity 22, reserve 2.5. PATH_04 energy 0.138944 kWh. After flight: 10.65855 - 0.138944 = 10.519606 > 2.5. Turnaround 1 slot at VP_05. Then post-service charge. After that, UAV_02 has MISSION_03 (index 1) from VP_05 to VP_01, release 9, due 12. So we need to depart M03 at slot 9 or later, arrive by 12. Path options for M03: PATH_01 (travel 4, COR_08), PATH_03 (travel 2, COR_07), PATH_05 (travel 1, COR_04). COR_07 is closed slots 9-32. So any departure slot for PATH_03 would use COR_07 in slots. For departure slot 9, travel 2 slots uses slots 9 and 10, both closed. So PATH_03 infeasible. PATH_01 uses COR_08, no closure. Travel 4 slots. Departure slot 9 would use 9,10,11,12, arriving 12, just meets due. Energy: 0.28992. So that works. PATH_05 uses COR_04, travel 1 slot. Departure 9 uses slot 9, arriving 9? Wait travel_slots=1, so depart 9, arrival slot 9? The travel time min unrounded 0.043, so 0 slots? The edge_segments says duration_slots 1. So travel 1 slot. If depart slot 9, then the flight uses slot 9, arrival at the end of slot 9? Typically arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots? The model: depart_slot + travel_slots = arrival slot? In baseline M03 depart 9 with PATH_03 travel 2, arrival presumably 11? But due slot 12, so arrival before due. For PATH_05 travel 1, departure 9 arrival 9? The due is 12, so okay. But need to check energy: PATH_05 energy 0.14496. So both PATH_01 and PATH_05 possible. The baseline used PATH_03 (closed now). So we can choose.\n\nBut there is also MISSION_05 for UAV_02, sequence 2, from VP_01 to VP_03, release 17, due 26, path PATH_09 (COR_02, travel 1 slot). So after M03, UAV_02 will be at VP_01, and need to depart at 17 or later. So we need to schedule M03 to finish by then, with charging possibly.\n\nNow, MISSION_02 for UAV_01: from VP_05 to VP_02, index 1, release 9, due 24. Path PATH_07 (COR_03, travel 1 slot). So departure slot 9 or later, arrival slot 9 (travel 1). Requires that UAV_01 has completed M01, turnaround 1 slot, and any post-service charging at VP_05. M01 arrives at slot 8 (depart 6, travel 3?). Actually PATH_02 travel 3 slots: depart 6, uses slots 6,7,8, arrival at end of slot 8? So arrival slot = 8? The due slot 8, so it's okay. Then turnaround at VP_05: 1 slot, so slot 9? The turnaround occurs at destination after service flight, so turnaround occupies slot 9? The normative order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So after service flight arriving at slot 8, turnaround in slot 9? Then post-service charge could start at slot 10. But MISSION_02 has release slot 9, can we depart at slot 9? The UAV availability: after turnaround, it's ready to depart? Actually, departure for next mission requires the UAV to be available after turnaround, and any post-service charging is optional and occurs before departure for next mission. The order says: \"post-service charge\" occurs at the destination after the stated turnaround. So for MISSION_01, if we do charge_blocks > 0, those charging slots occur after turnaround slot(s). If we want to depart MISSION_02 at slot 9, we must ensure that M01's turnaround finishes by slot 9 and no charging is done after that before departure? But the charge_blocks attached to M01 are at VP_05 after its service flight and turnaround, and they can be scheduled before M02 departure. The text: \"post-service charge occurs at that mission's destination after its service flight and the stated turnaround. It cannot power that mission's incoming deadhead, service flight, or reserve check. Its energy is available only to later missions assigned to the same UAV. At the start of a later mission, all legal post-service charging completed after earlier missions is already included in the UAV's available energy.\" So if we want the energy from charging after M01 to be available to M02, we need to schedule charging before M02 departure. But if we want to depart M02 at slot 9, then we cannot have any charging after M01 because turnaround at slot 9 would preclude charging at slot 9? The turnaround is 1 slot, so if M01 arrives at slot 8, turnaround occupies slot 9. Post-service charge can only happen after turnaround, i.e., starting slot 10. So if we want M02 to depart at slot 9, M02's departure would be at slot 9, which would overlap with M01's turnaround? The UAV cannot be doing two things: turnaround is part of M01's completion, and then M02's service flight. The departure slot of M02 is the slot in which the service flight starts. According to the sequence: M01's turnaround occupies slot 9. M02's service flight would need to start at slot 9? That conflicts: the UAV can't be in turnaround and departing at the same slot. So M02's departure must be after M01's turnaround ends. Since turnaround is 1 slot, M01 arrival slot 8 -> turnaround slot 9 -> ready next mission departure slot 10. But release slot for M02 is 9, so earliest departure is 10. due 24, so okay. So M02 depart slot 10 is possible. But check due? Arrival slot = 10+1=11, due 24, fine. So we can schedule M02 departure slot 10, which allows charging after M01 if we want. But do we need charging? M01's flight used 0.118406, leaving SOC ~1.538375. M02's service flight energy 0.086784, which would drain to ~1.451591, which is below reserve 1.5! So without charging, reserve check after M02's flight? Actually reserve check is after incoming deadhead + service flight. M02 has no incoming deadhead, so after service flight SOC = prior SOC - 0.086784. Prior SOC before M02 is whatever after M01 turnaround and any charging. If we do not charge, M01 end SOC = 1.538375. Then M02 flight: 1.538375 - 0.086784 = 1.451591 < 1.5, so reserve violation. So we must charge M01 enough to bring SOC above needed for M02 reserve. So we need at least enough to have after M02 flight SOC >= 1.5. So require prior SOC >= 1.5 + 0.086784 = 1.586784. So we need to add at least 1.586784 - 1.538375 = 0.048409 kWh. Each charge block gives 1.2 kWh (UAV_01 charge_kwh_per_block = 1.2). So one block adds 1.2, which is more than enough. So we can schedule 1 charge block after M01. That charge block would occupy slot 10? Post-service charge: we need to allocate charge_blocks, each block takes 1 slot? The charging time: presumably each block is one slot? The instance has max_charge_blocks 3. The baseline had 0 for all. So we can set charge_blocks = 1 for M01. That will charge at VP_05 starting after turnaround (slot 9). So charge slot 10? But if turnaround is slot 9, charging block 1 would be slot 10? Then M02 departure slot would need to be after charging ends. If we depart M02 at slot 11, then arrival 12, still fine (due 24). But release slot 9, so 11 is allowed. Let's see: M01 arrival 8, turnaround 9, charge block 10. Then UAV available at slot 11 for M02 departure. So M02 depart 11, arrival 11 (travel 1), due 24. Then M02 after flight: prior SOC after charging = 1.538375 + 1.2 = 2.738375 (but battery capacity is 16, so no overflow). Then minus M02 energy 0.086784 = 2.651591, >1.5 reserve. Turnaround at VP_02 1 slot, then post-service charge optional. After that, UAV_01 has no more missions. So done. So M01 path PATH_02, depart 6, charge_blocks 1. M02 path PATH_07, depart 11.\n\nNow check corridor capacity: M01 PATH_02 uses COR_05 slots 6,7,8. No capacity overrides there, default 2, okay. M02 PATH_07 uses COR_03, slot 11? No, travel slots 1, depart 11 uses slot 11, COR_03 capacity 2, no closure.\n\nNow UAV_02 missions: M04 (seq0) dep 6 PATH_04 (COR_01 slot 6, travel 1, arrival 6). Turnaround at VP_05 slot 7. Post-service charge? We need energy for M03 and M05. M04 after flight SOC: 10.65855 - 0.138944 = 10.519606. M03 flight energy options: PATH_01 0.28992, PATH_05 0.14496. Path selection: we need to meet due slot 12 and release 9. Also need reserve: after M03 flight, SOC must be >=2.5. M03 has no incoming deadhead. So prior SOC before M03 must be >= 2.5 + flight_energy. If we use PATH_05, need prior SOC >= 2.5+0.14496 = 2.64496. If PATH_01, need >= 2.5+0.28992 = 2.78992. After M04, SOC 10.5, so plenty. But we may want to charge to fill up for M05. However, after M03, we need M05 flight from VP_01 to VP_03, energy 0.44545. After M03, SOC will be prior minus flight_energy, then after M03 turnaround and post-service charge, we can charge before M05 departure. So we can charge at VP_01 after M03. So we could skip charging after M04 to save time. Then M03 departure: release 9. M04 turnaround at VP_05 slot 7, so UAV_02 is ready for M03 departure at slot 8? But M03 origin is VP_05, so after M04 arrival at VP_05, UEAV is at VP_05, turnaround 1 slot (slot 7). Then it can depart M03 at slot 8. Release is 9, so earliest 9, but 8 would violate release. So earliest departure slot is max(release, available). So departure slot must be >=9. So depart at 9. M03 path: we have PATH_01 (travel 4, arrive 12) or PATH_05 (travel 1, arrive 9). Both meet due 12. Let's evaluate energy: PATH_05 energy 0.14496, so after flight SOC = 10.519606 - 0.14496 = 10.374646 >2.5, ok. Then turnaround at VP_01 1 slot: arrival slot 9? If depart 9, travel 1, arrival at end of slot 9? So turnaround slot 10. Then we can charge post-service at VP_01. M05 release 17, due 26, travel 1. So we can depart M05 at 17 or later. So we need to ensure enough energy for M05. M05 energy 0.44545. After M03 (with PATH_05) SOC 10.374646, reserve check passes. Then turnaround slot 10, then we can charge. To serve M05, we need at least reserve after M05: need SOC after M05 >= 2.5. So prior SOC before M05 must be >= 2.5 + 0.44545 = 2.94545. We can charge multiple blocks at VP_01 after M03. Max charge blocks = 3 per mission. Each block gives 0.95 kWh. Starting from 10.374646, we need a little if any charging? Actually we already have well above 2.94545, so no charging needed for energy; but we may want to charge to fill battery for later? There's no later mission. So we could set charge_blocks=0 for M03. However, we need to consider post-service charge capacity at VP_01: charger capacity 2 slots. But not congested. So M03 path PATH_05 depart 9, charge_blocks 0, would be fine. But we need to check if M03's corridor COR_04 is closed? Not closed, capacity 2. So okay.\n\nAlternative: could use PATH_01 travel 4, depart 9 arrive 12, then turnaround at slot 13. Then M05 depart 17 or later. That works too, but uses more energy, so no need. So choose PATH_05 as it's faster and less energy.\n\nNow M05: sequence 2, path PATH_09 (COR_02), travel 1 slot, release 17, due 26. After M03 turnaround at VP_01 slot 10, UAV is ready at slot 11. So we can depart M05 as early as slot 17 (release), so depart 17, arrival 17 (travel 1). Energy prior: from after M03 charging? Since we set M03 charge_blocks=0, SOC before M05 is the SOC after M03 turnaround, which is 10.374646. Then M05 flight energy 0.44545, SOC after 9.929196 >2.5, reserve ok. Turnaround at VP_03 1 slot, then no more missions.\n\nSo schedule: M04: depart 6, path PATH_04, charge_blocks 0? Could charge after M04, but not needed. We can set charge_blocks=0. Turnaround 7, so ready slot 8. Then M03 depart 9? But M03 release 9, so ok. However, M03 uses COR_04 path PATH_05, travel 1, depart 9 arrival 9. But M04's turnaround at VP_05 slot 7, then UAV available slot 8, but M03 release is 9. So wait until 9. That's fine.\n\nBut we must ensure no corridor conflict: M03 uses COR_04 slot 9, capacity 2. M04 uses COR_01 slot 6. No overlap. Also check pad capacity: VP_04 pad capacity 2. M04 dep 6 uses pad slot 6? and M01 dep 6? M01 uses VP_04 as origin, departure slot 6, needs a pad. M04 also dep 6 from VP_04, so two missions departing same slot from VP_04, pad capacity 2, okay. Arrivals: M01 arrives VP_05 slot 8, M04 arrives VP_05 slot 6, no conflict. Also pad for M02 depart VP_05 slot 11? M02 depart slot 11, arriving VP_02 slot 11. VP_05 pad capacity 2, arrival may conflict with M01 departure? M02 dep 11, and M01 no longer at VP_05? M01 turned around at 9, then charged at 10 if charge block, then M01 finishes. But M01's charge block might need a charger at VP_05. Charger capacity 2. Slot 10: M01's charge block uses one